    "created_at": "2016-08-09T21:09:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-08-09T21:09:45Z",
    "body": "I'm not convinced that this changes anything.\n\nThe way I read the spec (long time ago...), `hgroup` has a \"titles\" and \"sub-titles\" - I implemented the display of titles only, as the actual display is not prescribed. This PR reasserts that there's \"primary\" and \"secondary\" headings. The spec as a whole also says that \"The `h1`–`h6` elements and the `hgroup` element are headings.\", i.e. `hgroup` as a whole is a heading - but nowhere does it say that an implementation should (must?) display the \"subheadings or subtitles or (secondary) alternative titles\", it only says \"How a user agent exposes such multi-level headings in user interfaces (e.g. in tables of contents or search results) is left open to implementors, as it is a user interface issue.\"\n\nMoreover, the W3C version has the `hgroup` deprecated and does not include it in the spec anymore...\n\nI'd say the note should point to something that is more prescriptive, rather than just mention that this is a multi-level heading and leave it at that, because this still leaves plenty of space for me to be lazy and not do anything about it :)\n\nHow should assistive technology implementations behave? Do screen readers even recognize `hgroup` at all - I haven't checked lately, but there used be ruckus that they don't use the outline algo at all? Would the people who use outlines actually care/differentiate between \"multi-level\" and regular headings? Do you expect all the sub-headings to be visible in TOC anyways? What's the actual user need here?\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
